---
id: PY-008
type: rule
family: python-reliability
title: eval() / exec() / compile() called with a dynamic argument
version: 1

severity: error
confidence: high
spectrum_position: 8

targets: [source]
file_extensions: [.py]
allowlist: ['**/test_*.py', '**/*_test.py', '**/tests/**', '**/conftest.py', '**/explain/**']

detection:
  type: regex
  pattern: '\b(eval|exec|compile)\s*\(\s*[A-Za-z_]'

related: [AP-008, PY-009]
enabled: true
opt_in: false
---

Don't call `eval()`, `exec()`, or `compile(..., "exec")` with a dynamic
argument. The first character inside the parens is an identifier, which means
the string being executed is not a static literal and may be attacker-controlled
at runtime.

Replace with a structured dispatch — a dict of callables keyed on the input, a
pydantic / attrs schema for data validation, or a restricted interpreter
(`ast.literal_eval` for literals only, RestrictedPython, a subprocess with a
clean env) if you genuinely need to run untrusted code.

If the argument is a string literal you control end-to-end (e.g. `eval("1 + 1")`),
the rule skips you when the leading character inside the parens is a quote.
